<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Zaghawa sultanates</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1916 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A cluster of small Zaghawa sultanates on the Darfur-Chad frontier, including Kobe, Dar Tuar and Dar Gala, caught between Darfur and Wadai.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaghawa">Zaghawa</a> are a Saharan people of the borderlands between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, mentioned by Arabic writers from the 9th century. By the 17th century several Zaghawa chieftaincies had formed sultanates, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Kobe</a>, Dar Tuar and Dar Gala, in the semi-desert north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultanates were small, with rulers drawn from leading lineages governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a>-herding clans and some farming communities. They paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/darfur-sultanate">Darfur Sultanate</a> or the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire</a> and supplied them with soldiers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread among them, mixed with older practices, and they controlled routes toward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French troops occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a> from 1909, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a> in 1916. The Anglo-French border settlement split the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaghawa">Zaghawa</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, and their sultans became traditional chiefs under colonial rule.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1600 CE – 1916 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.54%;width:6.29%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1916 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Zaghawa (Beria)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sudan">Sudan</a>, <a href="/countries/chad">Chad</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Dar Zaghawa, Kobe, Dar Tuar, Dar Gala</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1758&amp;focus=zaghawa-sultanates" aria-label="Zaghawa sultanates on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5483 1578 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zaghawa sultanates lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5695 1926h0M5571 1919h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5803 1834h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5803 1834h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Sudan. <a href="/atlas?year=1758&amp;focus=zaghawa-sultanates">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

